Subject: [PATCH 00/88] MT6768/even: vendor 4.19.325 delta on v5.15.220 - boot path (clocks, PMIC, ADC, supplies, eMMC, DTB packaging), MT6768 SMI substrate, M4U v2.0 engine without ION, and its first display client

Zenium 4.19.325 -> v5.15.220 for the Realme "even" (MT6769 / MT6768 family) board.

What this series is: the mechanically portable vendor delta (0001-0068), then the
device-specific work that makes a mainline-based kernel build a boot-shaped image: image
packaging kbuild (0069), the board device-tree transplant - a 55-file include closure,
pinctrl-mt6768 (0071), devapc (0072), an RTC rework revert (0073), the MT6768 clock
provider plus MTCMOS power gating (0074), the PMIC chain - pwrap for MT6768 with the
MT6358 node-name alias that lets the MFD, its regulator cell and the RTC driver bind
(0075), both AUXADC providers, resolvable PMIC supply phandles and the eMMC host (0076),
a kbuild fix so the DTB the image actually carries is the one that was audited (0077) -
then the multimedia address-translation substrate: the vendor MT6768 SMI local-arbiter
driver (0078), renamed so M4U's own conditionals select it (0079), the MT6768 M4U v2.0
engine ported without ION and without dma-buf heaps (0080), and the first real client of
that engine, the display-side M4U glue, in this cover's 0081, and the CMDQ
revert that makes the mailbox stack self-consistent in 0082, plus the four CMDQ client
entry points the display path calls and 5.15 lacks in 0083, and the first display slice -
the CMDQ-free dispsys core - in 0084, the include-set repair (0085) that makes the whole video
directory build again, the wait/pagemap include fix (0086) that a whole-tree build was needed to
find, the Kconfig gate (0087) that makes every tip link an image again, and the CMDQ
backup-slot pool (0088) that the landed display core calls on directly.

Every hardware claim in 0074-0083 is cited to register-, field- or call-site level data

0082 is a revert, and it exists because 0034 was wrong rather than incomplete. 0034 carried the
4.19 vendor mailbox driver's +460 lines into mainline's drivers/mailbox/mtk-cmdq-mailbox.c; those
lines reference cmdq_err, CMDQ_DRIVER_NAME, cmdq_msg and struct cmdq's base_pa member - names that
belong to vendor engine code this series does not carry. With CONFIG_MTK_CMDQ_MBOX enabled the tree
therefore did not build, and because no fragment had ever enabled that symbol the breakage stayed
invisible for seven builds (0078-0081 all reported zero errors with the file simply absent from the
build). 0082 restores mtk-cmdq-mailbox.c, include/linux/mailbox/mtk-cmdq-mailbox.h and
drivers/mailbox/Makefile to v5.15.220, so mainline's CMDQ stack is coherent: one packet model, one
header. drivers/mailbox/mailbox.c keeps its +39-line 0034 delta on purpose - grepping that hunk for
cmdq or gce returns nothing, so it is a generic mailbox-core carry and has no part in the
incoherence. include/linux/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq.h needed no change: git checkout v5.15.220 -- on
it reported it byte-identical to upstream, which is why the collision was confined to C files.
Measured with CONFIG_MTK_CMDQ_MBOX=y on the recreated v5.15.220 tree:
make -j2 drivers/mailbox/ drivers/soc/mediatek/ returns rc=0, producing mtk-cmdq-mailbox.o
(123,688 B) and mtk-cmdq-helper.o (92,776 B), and all four unresolved-identifier errors are gone.

0083 adds the four CMDQ client entry points that the 21 objects this board actually builds from
video/mt6768/dispsys/ call and that v5.15.220 does not provide: cmdq_dev_get_event (7 callsites),
cmdq_pkt_wait_no_clear (3), cmdq_pkt_flush (2) and cmdq_pkt_flush_threaded (1). They land in
mainline's own drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-helper.c, and are declared in mainline's
include/linux/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq.h, because struct cmdq_instruction and the CMDQ_CODE_*/CMDQ_WFE_*
encodings are private to that file and the tree must keep exactly one CMDQ client API.
cmdq_dev_get_event is the vendor's function ported as-is (gce-event-names, then gce-events with
#gce-event-cells, returning the first cell) with pr_err() replacing the vendor's cmdq_err() so the
null-dev path cannot dereference dev; it resolves DT properties this port's device tree does not
carry yet, so on even it returns an error for now and the display round must supply them.
cmdq_pkt_wait_no_clear introduces no encoding: 4.19.325 appends a WFE with
arg_b = CMDQ_WFE_WAIT | CMDQ_WFE_WAIT_VALUE and arg_c zero, and in this tree CMDQ_WFE_OPTION is
exactly that pair with CMDQ_WFE_UPDATE added only when cmdq_pkt_wfe() is told to clear, so
cmdq_pkt_wfe(pkt, event, false) emits the identical instruction. cmdq_pkt_flush is where stock and
mainline really differ and the patch says so in place: the vendor blocks on a completion taken from
the EOC callback, but this tree's cmdq_pkt_flush_async() calls mbox_client_txdone() itself right
after sending ("We can send next packet immediately, so just call txdone"), so such a completion
would already be done at submit time and would not wait for the GCE at all; the port finalizes and
blocks on mbox_flush(), which cmdq_mbox_flush() implements by draining the thread's pending list,
and that returns -ETIMEDOUT where the vendor would have hung. CMDQ_PKT_FLUSH_TIMEOUT_MS is a policy
choice, labelled as such, not a measured value. cmdq_pkt_flush_threaded keeps stock's contract
(queue, run the caller's callback in process context, -ENOMEM if the allocation fails).
Verified with the device config: make -j2 ARCH=arm64 CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-buildroot-linux-gnu-
drivers/mailbox/ drivers/soc/mediatek/ returns rc=0 with no errors and no warnings, and nm over
every object built so far shows each of the four symbols defined exactly once, referenced by nothing
yet because no display file is ported. cmdq_pkt_sleep and cmdq_pkt_sleep_by_poll are still open: the
opcode enum in include/linux/mailbox/mtk-cmdq-mailbox.h has no SLEEP, LOGIC or plain READ code, so
their instruction words were to be transcribed from the vendor rather than written from analogy -
that is now settled by measurement and they are NOT ported: a comment-stripping, guard-resolving
census (bin/cmdqcensus.py) shows 0 live callsites for cmdq_pkt_sleep (its only occurrence,
ddp_dsi.c:7099, sits inside a /* */ comment) and 0 for cmdq_pkt_sleep_by_poll (all 4 callsites and
the vendor definition and declaration sit under #ifdef CONFIG_MTK_MT6382_BDG, unset on even), while
cmdq_pkt_poll_gpr_check() is wholly inside #if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_MACH_MT6885). Adding them would be
unreachable code, so instead tests/cmdq_words_host_check.c transcribes both trees' encoders and
compares the emitted 64-bit words: 48 comparisons, 0 mismatches, which machine-confirms the
wait_no_clear bit-identity claim above. cmdq_register_device stays unshimmed; ddp_disp_bdg.c
rewrites onto cmdq_dev_get_client_reg() when that file lands.

0084 opens the display path (L2) with the 14 dispsys objects that compile without any v3 record-layer
implementation, so the display core can be compiled and reviewed before the v3 record-layer question is decided. Every
source is copied verbatim from 4.19.325 except four marked adaptations, each the minimal mainline
equivalent of something v5.15 removed: struct timeval -> struct timespec64 in the carried v3 headers
(mainline dropped that type from kernel headers entirely), do_gettimeofday() -> ktime_get_real_ts64()
with the vendor's microsecond arithmetic preserved, and proc_create()'s fourth argument moved to
struct proc_ops for the three /proc entries while the debugfs entries keep the vendor file_operations.
ddp_log.h is now carried at the vendor's full 112 lines instead of the 51-line copy 0081 needed,
because ddp_mutex.c uses DISP_LOG_W and DDPDUMP. The include closure of these objects reaches the v3
CMDQ header set (cmdq_record.h, cmdq_def.h, cmdq_event_common.h, cmdq_core.h, cmdq_subsys_common.h,
cmdq_helper_ext.h, mt6768/cmdq_engine.h) and so 46 headers land; they are headers only - "find
drivers/misc/mediatek/cmdq -name '*.c'" returns nothing - which is why this does not re-open the
one-client-API problem that 0082 closed, and no v3 engine behaviour is claimed.
Deliberately excluded: ddp_dsi, ddp_ovl, ddp_rdma_ex, ddp_wdma_ex, ddp_mmp, ddp_disp_bdg (record API /
BDG paths) and ddp_path, whose three record-API calls would put a link dependency inside a slice meant
to be record-free - its header is still carried, because the closure resolves through it.
Verification limit as it stood for that patch: it was a compile gate, not a link gate - the sandbox held
1,772 of roughly 7,400 objects at the time, so no vmlinux link and no full image was claimed at 0084
(the gate gained its whole-tree link stage in 0087, and every slice since is gated with it). What was measured: rc=0 with
zero errors from a directory whose .o files were deleted first; all 14 expected objects present and
non-empty (sizes in the commit message); 0 duplicate link-visible definitions across the slice; and an
nm -u census against the 79,730 symbols defined in this partial tree that leaves ddp_color_format.o
fully resolved, while ddp_mutex.o and ddp_rsz.o need cmdqRecWrite (reached through register-write
macros in ddp_reg.h, not through source text), 7 names belong to the excluded ddp_path.o, and the rest
are L3/L4 providers such as ddp_get_module_num, primary_display_diagnose and mtkfb_*. So the slice is
compile-clean and explicitly not self-linking; the record layer remains the next decision.

The CMDQ work is not finished by this patch and 0082 does not pretend otherwise. Counting only the
21 objects this board actually builds from video/mt6768/dispsys/Makefile (ddp_disp_bdg.o is gated on
CONFIG_MACH_MT6768, not on CONFIG_MTK_MT6382_BDG, so its callsites are live), the display path needs
14 entry points. 5.15 provides cmdq_pkt_write, cmdq_pkt_clear_event, cmdq_pkt_create,
cmdq_pkt_destroy, cmdq_pkt_flush_async, cmdq_pkt_poll, cmdq_mbox_create; it does not provide
cmdq_dev_get_event (7 callsites), cmdq_pkt_wait_no_clear (3), cmdq_pkt_sleep_by_poll (3),
cmdq_pkt_flush (2) or cmdq_pkt_sleep (1). Three names in my earlier list - cmdq_pkt_write_masked,
cmdq_pkt_wait, cmdq_pkt_event_clear - have zero occurrences anywhere in the vendor tree and are not
requirements at all. The provider in stock even is not cmdq/v3 (33,968 lines of GCE engine, of which
the display path uses nothing) but MTK's extended copy of mainline's own
drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-helper.c (2,521 lines, 97 functions, 84 absent from 5.15); that file
is where the missing symbols must land, because CMDQ_WFE_* and struct cmdq_instruction are private
to it. cmdq_register_device is deliberately not shimmed: it returns struct cmdq_base built from
gce-subsys / gce-cpr-range properties that this port's device tree does not carry, and the single
live callsite is to be replaced with 5.15's verified cmdq_dev_get_client_reg() when that file is
ported.

from the BSP itself (offset tables, vendor of_match entries, the board's own defconfig and
its Makefile conditionals, DT child-name matching).  Where a subsystem was NOT enabled the
reason is in the commit message: I2C is a device-tree binding gap (the board DT's nine i2cN
nodes are the BSP's legacy hardware description, not adapters), so no CONFIG was flipped to
imply otherwise.

0081 in one paragraph.  The display tree in 4.19 is 647,104 lines of C (71,401 of it
MT6768), so the client was found by tracing the one boot-visible chain that reaches
m4u_alloc_mva() - the LK-logo handover, videox/mtkfb.c:2648 -> dispsys/ddp_m4u.c:338 - and
porting only that file plus the option table it reads: 10 files, 1,116 lines (plus one line each in drivers/misc/{Kconfig,Makefile}), 2 objects,
CONFIG_MTK_DISP_M4U=y, no device tree edit and no new DT binding (the client has no
of_device_id table, and binding mediatek,dispsys with a partial driver would switch four
display ports to virtual addressing while LK keeps scanning the logo, so this is gated and
deliberately uncalled).  All 41 ion_ references in the vendor file sit inside seven
disp_ion_*() wrappers whose bodies the vendor's own MTK_FB_ION_SUPPORT gate already
compiles out of a kernel build; the wrappers and the ION types their prototypes need are
removed, and CONFIG_DMABUF_HEAPS stays unset.  Four vendor dependencies (ddp_reg.h and its
cmdq/display_recorder closure, ddp_dump.c, ddp_info.c's module-name table, and the
dprec/ddb_debug log layer) were resolved by leaving them out, each with the reason in the
source; the resulting differences - no /dev/pmsg/dprec mirror, no register dump in the fault
callback - are documented rather than hidden.  The trace also recorded two vendor facts
worth knowing before the core lands: MT6768 runs disp_m4u_init() (ddp_drv.c:557) before
disp_helper_option_init() (ddp_drv.c:593) while DISP_OPT_USE_M4U defaults to 0, and
config_display_m4u_port() leaves struct m4u_port_config_struct.domain uninitialised.

0085 is a build fix, and it exists because a defect in 0084 was invisible to the gates: 0084 replaced
dispsys/display_recorder.h with the vendor file, which includes mmprofile.h (drivers/misc/mediatek/mmp/)
and, through ddp_info.h, ion.h. videox/Makefile never listed mmp/ in its -I set, and videox/ has carried
`obj-y += disp_helper.o` since 0081 - disp_helper.c includes that same display_recorder.h. So from 0084
on, `make ARCH=arm64 drivers/misc/mediatek/video/` died with `fatal error: mmprofile.h: No such file or
directory`, while every gate that named .../dispsys/ stayed green, because the dispsys Makefile does
advertise mmp/. The series was slice-green and tree-broken at the same time. 0085 regenerates
videox/Makefile with the include set l2slice.py computes for dispsys and changes nothing else: exactly
one file, no source touched. The process fix matters more than the -I lines - l2-slice-gate.sh now builds
the parent directory and derives the expected objects from the obj-y of every landed Makefile in the
slice, so "the directory under review compiles" can no longer stand in for "the tree builds".

0086 and 0087 are the first patches in this series that could only be found by building and linking the
WHOLE tree, and both are recorded in report/l2-wholetree-survey.md. 0086 removes a line 0001 carried from
the vendor's include/linux/wait.h - `#include <linux/sched/debug.h>`, inert in 4.19 but in 5.15 the route by
which mainline's `static inline void show_state(void)` reaches every translation unit, where it collides
with drivers/acpi/fan.c's `DEVICE_ATTR_RW(state)` callback of the same name and kills the build. Both
colliding files are byte-identical to mainline in this series, and pristine v5.15.220 compiles fan.o with
the same .config, so the cause is the carried line; the line is also load-bearing (deleting it fails
`make prepare`, because the vendor added `__sched` - defined only in sched/debug.h - to pagemap.h,
mm/filemap.c and kernel/sched/wait.c), so the fix states the dependency in pagemap.h, where it is used, and
lets wait.h go back byte-identical to mainline. 0087 then gates the landed display objects behind
`CONFIG_MTK_DISP_BRINGUP` (default n), which is how the vendor gates the same directory
(`video/mt6768/Makefile:20: obj-$(CONFIG_MTK_FB) += dispsys/`): with 0084's objects in plain obj-y the tree
compiled but `vmlinux` failed on 507 undefined references - the provider gap the compile gate had already
counted as 87 names - a count that moves with how much of the tree is built, so it is always quoted
with its build state - and so no image could be produced from 0084 through 0086. With the gate off the tree
links and packages: vmlinux 167,987,640 B, Image.gz-dtb 12,204,090 B, appended DTB payload 493,517 B
(build-37's recorded size), mt6768.dtb still 122,474 B / sha 34a7e6b536a3, unchanged since build-33.
Enabling the switch compiles the 15 ported objects (15/15, 0 error lines) and does NOT make display output
work: 220 names are still unresolved with the switch on and 67 of them have no provider anywhere in the
tree at this build state (3,709 objects), the record layer is still an open architectural question, and the
panel is still not enabled by the driver.

0088 closes the one part of that gap that could be closed without deciding the record layer. ddp_drv.c
allocates and reads backup slots directly - disp_probe_1() (ddp_drv.c:499) calls cmdqBackupAllocateSlot
for DISP_SLOT_NUM=5 (ddp_hal.h:88), ddp_manager.c:415/:545 read slots back - and 5.15 has no such API.
The patch adds the pool to drivers/soc/mediatek/ (mtk-cmdq-disp-slot.c/.h), keyed on the same
CONFIG_MTK_DISP_BRINGUP switch, which is also what makes 0087's default-off state hold: with the switch
off the provider is not built, vmlinux is byte-for-byte the 0087 image inputs, and with it on the
whole-tree link goes from 507 to 502 undefined-reference lines - exactly those three names - while the
29 cmdqRecWrite references stay untouched. It reproduces what the vendor does, defects included: the
lookup finds the pool containing base+idx*4 and does not bound idx by that pool's slot_count, so an
out-of-range index reads or writes the next pool (or a NULL pool's base + offset) rather than failing -
the host harness in tests/mtk_disp_slot_host_check.c runs stock and port side by side over 37 cases and
gets 0 mismatches, including the aliasing case, whose value lands in the neighbouring pool on BOTH sides;
the vendor's own guard (cmdq_helper_ext.c:2163, which logs and drops out-of-range writes) is deliberately
not carried because the landed client does not reach it, so a future caller would write where the vendor
would drop. cmdqBackupFree and the vendor cmdq_alloc_mem/cmdq_cpu_vaddr layer are not ported (no reference
to them in the landed objects, nm -u measured).

What 0088 also settles is the size of the remaining blocker, measured rather than assumed. cmdqRecWrite
is referenced 29 times at link time from 2 objects (ddp_mutex.o, ddp_rsz.o, through the DISP_REG_* macros
in ddp_reg.h:115) and nothing in the landed set creates a record: nm -u finds 2 cmdqRecWrite references
and 0 to cmdqRecCreate/cmdqRecDestroy, and the handle ddp_manager forwards is a parameter, not something
it allocates. So the record write is a link-time requirement on the next slice, not something a device
would execute today; the GCE mailbox binding question stays open, and no compatible string, #mbox-cells
or port-local provider was invented for it.

